# THIS FILE MUST NOT SHIP EITHER. `COPY . /work` copies .dockerignore itself, and this file is a
# precise MAP OF EVERYTHING WE ARE HIDING: it names the wolf3d BFS solver and the `tileMap`
# property it reads, states the wolf3d target (9309f), the SML human gold (6017f) and the fastest
# known MK64 Wario run (7561f), and lists the exact archive paths holding every prior result. An
# agent that reads it learns both the frontier and where the answers used to live — so the
# denylist would leak precisely what the denylist exists to withhold. Excluding it costs nothing:
# .dockerignore is consumed by the BUILDER from the context root, never needed inside the image.
.dockerignore

# Keep the host (macOS) venv out — the container builds its own linux venv.
.venv/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc

# VCS / editor
.git/
.gitignore
.opencode/log/

# Heavy / irrelevant to the agent
media/
logs/
*.mp4
*.mov
results/**/*.mp4
notebooks/
node_modules/

# Run OUTPUTS must NOT be baked into the image — they are runtime artifacts mounted at
# /work/exported via a volume. Baking out/ in lets one run's exported trajectories/candidates
# leak into another run's image (e.g. a no-emulator open-loop run could read a closed-loop
# run's validated results), contaminating the experiment.
out/

# Secrets must NOT be baked into the image (passed via env at runtime).
# NOTE: .dockerignore patterns are NOT recursive, so `.env` / `*.env` only ever matched a
# ROOT-level file — and the only .env in this repo is sandbox/.env, which was therefore being
# baked into every agent image (BASETEN/OPENAI/GEMINI keys + EVAL_RESET_SECRET, the secret that
# resets the hardened eval-service per-turn budget). `**/.env` matches it at any depth.
.env
*.env
**/.env
**/*.env
.tools/

# --- RESULT ARTIFACTS MUST NOT SHIP IN THE AGENT IMAGE ---
# The repo is COPYed into the agent image, so anything here is readable by the agent. A cold-start
# run read sandbox/mk64_frontier_history/ and .opencode/skills/mk64-tas-techniques/SKILL.md and
# lifted the answer straight out: "the sandwich technique produced the fastest known Wario run at
# 7561 frames", then used our own best trajectories as graft donors. The prompt was clean; the
# IMAGE was not.
sandbox/mk64_frontier_history/
sandbox/mk64_runs_archive/
sandbox/civ_hof/
sandbox/figures/
sandbox/mk64_slowmo_lap.json

# scripts/mk64_shortcut/ -- the START-LEFT wall-jump toolkit AND its README, which states
# outright: "The shortcut *completes a lap* (`frontier.json`, goal_frame ~14,593) but is ~1.9x
# slower". Found being read by the mk64c fleet on 2026-09-21: the agents' own PLAN.md came back
# citing "a prior session built the start-left jump (frontier.json, goal ~14,593f)". That is our
# result, handed to an arm whose whole point is to discover things unaided. The code is kept in
# the repo -- it is real work -- it just must not ship inside the agent image.
scripts/mk64_shortcut/

# scripts/preflight_run.sh -- the LEAK DETECTOR, which is itself a leak. Line 125 greps the
# agent for "2080|2112|2150|6877|7703|7561", so the file states six records in plain text.
# It is a HOST-side tool (it runs `docker exec "$AGENT" ...`); it has no function inside the
# image it is checking. Same failure as .dockerignore itself, which is excluded two lines up
# for exactly this reason: the denylist leaks what the denylist exists to withhold.
scripts/preflight_run.sh

# --- SUPER MARIO LAND result artifacts (added for the ORACLE_FEEDBACK arms) ---
# results/speedrun/ was emptied in the working tree and its 64 prior trajectories were MOVED to
# sandbox/sml_runs_archive/ — which was NOT ignored, so every one of them (the whole optimized
# search history, the human gold, plus ISSUE.md / README.md / REFINEMENT_STRATEGY.md describing
# the search) was still being baked into the agent image. A "from scratch" arm could simply read
# the finished answer. The SEEDED arms get the human gold injected at RUN TIME via a read-only
# bind mount (see the oracle2 profile in sandbox/docker-compose.yml), never from the image.
sandbox/sml_runs_archive/
sandbox/seeds/
sandbox/coldstart_exported/
sandbox/RESULTS_SUMMARY_*.md
# --- WOLFENSTEIN 3D E1L1 (wolf3d) — THE ANSWER AND THE ANSWER-GENERATOR MUST NOT SHIP ---
# The wolf3d arms (4 models x scratch/seeded) all run from ONE shared image, so anything left
# here reaches the from-scratch arms too. Two repo artifacts hand over the finished level:
#   1. scripts/wolf3d_route.py is a BFS SOLVER: it reads the game's `tileMap` (the level tile
#      grid), locates the exit and EMITS a complete winning route. An arm that can run it never
#      has to search at all.
#   2. sandbox/wolf3d_e1l1_route.json IS the finished, validated 9309-frame route.
# Seeded arms are entitled to (2) — but they receive it at RUN TIME via a read-only bind mount
# declared in the compose file, never baked into the image that scratch arms also boot from.
#
# PATTERN FORM MATTERS: .dockerignore patterns are NOT recursive. `sandbox/wolf3d_e1l1_route.json`
# on its own matched only that one exact path — the same class of bug that shipped sandbox/.env
# for months because `.env` only ever matched a root-level file (see the note above). Every rule
# below therefore gets both its exact path and a `**/` form, so a copy that lands under any
# other directory is still excluded.
scripts/wolf3d_route.py
**/wolf3d_route.py
sandbox/wolf3d_e1l1_route.json
**/wolf3d_e1l1_route.json
**/*wolf3d*route*
**/*wolf3d*solve*
**/*route*wolf3d*
# A third leak: the host launcher names the solver in its rsync excludes AND states the target
# in plain text (SEED_FRAMES=9309), so a scratch arm that read it would learn the bar to beat.
scripts/launch_wolf3d_arms.sh
**/launch_wolf3d_arms.sh
# The E3L9 boss-probe env/scorer (feasibility study, no arm runs from them). wolf3d_boss_env.py
# ships a TILEMAP dumper — the same `tileMap` read that got wolf3d_route.py excluded above — so
# it must not land in the shared wolf3d image either. No live arm needs these files.
scripts/wolf3d_boss_env.py
scripts/wolf3d_boss_score.py
**/wolf3d_boss_env.py
**/wolf3d_boss_score.py
# (sandbox/figures/ is already excluded above; it holds plot_wolf3d.py and A_run_log.csv, which
# tabulate achieved wolf3d frame counts. Noted here so the wolf3d reasons live in one place.)
# Raw agent session transcripts: plain-text tables of every SML result.
# `**/` IS LOAD-BEARING. A bare `2026-*.txt` matches only the repo ROOT, so
# sandbox/2026-08-21-...-continued-from-a-previous-c.txt (1.2MB, quotes achieved pk scores) shipped
# to every agent container. Verified visible inside a running arm before this was widened.
**/2026-*.txt

# sandbox/patches/ is HOST-SIDE engine-patch documentation and nothing in the repo imports it, but
# patches/tuxemon/README.md:30 records a validation run against `tapes_by_model/glm53_best_98116f
# .json` and states `engine_steps=98116`. That is a PRIOR RUN'S RECORD, handed to every arm whose
# BRIEF says the bar is 105,400. Confirmed read: tuxmap-fable quoted those two lines verbatim in
# its turn-1 output, then finished first at 96,151 -- i.e. the leaked number acted as a target.
# The TAPE itself never leaked (tux_tas is mounted into the eval-service only), so this gave a
# frontier, not a solution -- but it contaminates any claim that a score was discovered unaided.
sandbox/patches/
# plots/ is a LEADERBOARD IN MACHINE-READABLE FORM and must never ship. pk_all_series.json is a
# dict keyed by MODEL NAME ('kimi', 'opus', 'glm', 'gpt-sol', 'ds-flash', 'ds-pro', 'grok',
# 'glm-5.3') whose values are the full per-turn score series; plot_pokemon.py hardcodes the same
# numbers. An arm that reads either learns the achievable frontier AND every rival's trajectory.
# Excluded wholesale rather than per-file: this directory accumulates new series per experiment,
# so an allowlist would silently go stale the next time a plot is added.
plots/
# Prose that quotes achieved SML frame counts (the frontier a from-scratch arm must not be told).
sandbox/ONLINE_SPEEDRUN.md
sandbox/ARTIFACTS.md
# RESEARCH_PROPOSAL.md tabulates the findings of the earlier SML batches, frame counts included.
RESEARCH_PROPOSAL.md
# TUXEMON_ANALYSIS.md names the tux fleet's achieved scores (98,116 / 99,226) AND the specific
# inefficiency worth exploiting -- four redundant whiteout laps, ~60k steps -- which is precisely
# what the tuxtrail arms are supposed to discover for themselves.
TUXEMON_ANALYSIS.md
# AGENT_GLOSSARY.md distils every model's TECHNIQUE into one page -- bisection, broadcast,
# witness/licensing, mash-period floors, fallback axes. It names few scores, but shipping it
# would hand each arm the collective playbook and destroy the strategy-diversity result that
# the cross-model comparison exists to measure.
AGENT_GLOSSARY.md
# POKEMON_ANALYSIS.md is the same hazard for the pk arms and MUST NOT ship. It names an ACHIEVED
# score (glm53's 126755, far under the 160021 the agent is shown as its bar), says which boundary
# strategies paid off, and documents the savestate WRAM decode that condition B exists to remove.
# `COPY . /work` (sandbox/Dockerfile:105) would hand every arm the answer and the other models'
# playbooks in one file.
POKEMON_ANALYSIS.md
# The compose file is the experiment DESIGN (arm names, seeding, the gold's frame count) and is
# never needed inside an agent container — only on the host that runs `docker compose`.
sandbox/docker-compose.yml
sandbox/docker-compose.yml.bak*
# Non-oracle SML agent cards quote an achievable frame RANGE. The oracle cards
# (.opencode/agent/sml-tas-*-oracle.md) are clean and are the only ones these arms need.
# They also name specific prior best trajectories ("fused-glm-5.2_sml_1-1_<N>f.json (~<N>f)").
# Drop every SML card, then re-admit ONLY the *-oracle ones these arms actually run.
# NOTE: this also removes them from the legacy `agent` / `glm-agent` / `mimo-agent` compose
# services' images; those point at retired endpoints and are not in use.
.opencode/agent/sml-tas*.md
!.opencode/agent/sml-tas-*-oracle.md
# --- GameWorld 03_astray — THE ROUTE AND THE ROUTE-GENERATOR MUST NOT SHIP ---
# The 4 astray arms are all FROM SCRATCH and all boot the ONE shared astray-tas-oracle image,
# so anything left here reaches every one of them. scripts/astray_route.py reads window.maze
# (the wall grid), BFSes to the exit and drives a closed-loop controller that EMITS a finished
# [key, frames] action list — an arm that can run it never has to search at all. Any saved
# route is the answer itself.
#
# HONEST SCOPE — what this does and does NOT contain. It withholds (a) the finished route,
# (b) the tool that generates one, and (c) every achieved frame count. It does NOT withhold
# the maze layout: scripts/astray_score.py's min_dist_cells feedback is a real BFS geodesic,
# so scripts/astray_env.py (which must ship, the scorer imports it) necessarily contains the
# code that reads window.maze. That containment would be theatre anyway — the game itself is
# bind-mounted into every arm and the agent has bash and a browser, so an agent that wants the
# wall grid can always read it out of the game. Discovering the maze is legitimate play here;
# being handed a finished trajectory is not. Claiming more than this would be a lie.
#
# PATTERN FORM MATTERS: .dockerignore patterns are NOT recursive (this is the bug that shipped
# sandbox/.env for months), so every rule gets both its exact path and a `**/` form.
scripts/astray_route.py
**/astray_route.py
**/*astray*route*
**/*astray*solve*
**/*route*astray*
**/*astray*[0-9]f.json

# ============================================================================================
# STRUCTURAL HARDENING — CLASS RULES, NOT PER-INCIDENT ONE-OFFS
# ============================================================================================
# Everything above this line was written one leak at a time: a specific solver, a specific
# route file, a specific archive directory. That is why the E3L9 and Grok builds each needed
# their OWN denylist (sandbox/Dockerfile.e3l9.dockerignore, sandbox/Dockerfile.grokw3d.
# dockerignore) before they could ship — the root list left sandbox/assert_e3l9_arm.py,
# sandbox/docker-compose.e3l9.yml and sandbox/Dockerfile.e3l9* in the image, and those three
# quote the E1L1 solver, the `tileMap` read and the literal 9309-frame target in plain text.
# A build that FORGOT to point at a private denylist therefore shipped the answer.
#
# The rules below are by CLASS, so a new experiment's plumbing is excluded the day it is
# written rather than the day someone notices it leaked. Every rule carries BOTH a root-anchored
# and a `**/` form, because .dockerignore patterns are NOT recursive (the bug that shipped
# sandbox/.env for months).
#
# NONE of this is needed inside an agent container: the Dockerfile is read by the BUILDER from
# its own path (never through the context copy), compose runs on the HOST, and the assert_*_arm
# gates are bind-mounted READ-ONLY at run time precisely so the image carries no description of
# what is being withheld.

# --- 1. Build / experiment plumbing (names arms, seeding, targets, and the withheld files) ---
Dockerfile*
**/Dockerfile*
# Trailing-suffix forms matter: a bare `*.dockerignore` does NOT match `.dockerignore.bak`,
# and that backup was still shipping (it is an older copy of this very denylist).
.dockerignore*
*.dockerignore*
**/.dockerignore*
**/*.dockerignore*
docker-compose*
**/docker-compose*
# The COMPOSE GENERATORS and launch scripts carry the same experiment-design prose as the compose
# files they emit -- prior self-eval counts, cross-model standings, achieved scores -- and are
# equally host-only. Excluding `docker-compose*` alone was not enough: gen_pknostate.py and
# gen_tuxtrail.py were verified VISIBLE inside a running arm, quoting exactly the material the
# compose exclusion was written to withhold. Anything that generates or launches an experiment
# belongs on the host, never in the image.
sandbox/gen_*.py
sandbox/launch_*.sh
**/gen_pknostate.py
**/gen_tuxtrail.py
# The turn-0 containment gates. They enumerate, in plain text, every artifact and every content
# pattern that is being kept out — i.e. a full map of the answer. Each one reaches the arm that
# needs it via a read-only bind mount, never via the image.
assert_*_arm.py
**/assert_*_arm.py
# CLASS RULE, same argument, wider pattern. The rule above matched assert_*_ARM.py only, so
# scripts/assert_astray_seeded.py shipped in the shared image with the astray seed baseline as a
# literal default (--frames 1697). A from-scratch astray arm greps its filesystem and finds the
# number it is supposed to discover. Seeded arms get their assertion by BIND MOUNT (see the
# ./assert_e3l9_arm.py:/assert/assert_arm.py:ro pattern), so nothing needs it baked in.
assert_*_seeded.py
**/assert_*_seeded.py
scripts/assert_astray_seeded.py

# --- 2. Secrets, at any depth (see the .env note higher up) ---
**/.env
**/*.env
**/.env.*

# --- 3. Vendored JS, at any depth. `node_modules/` alone matched only the ROOT copy, so
#        .opencode/node_modules (36k files) was being baked into every image.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# --- 4. SOLVERS AND ROUTE GENERATORS, BY CLASS ---------------------------------------------
# A "route generator" is any tool that reads the level out of the game and EMITS a trajectory.
# Handing one to an arm removes the search that the benchmark is measuring. Named rules for the
# known ones already exist above; these are the class rules that also catch the next one.
**/*_route.py
**/*_solver.py
**/*_solve.py
scripts/*route*.py
scripts/*solv*.py
# The published-TAS replay drivers. They exist to re-run HappyLee's SMB movies and state the
# movie ids and their frame counts in their own docstrings, so they are both a route source and
# a frontier disclosure. sandbox/Dockerfile.smb.dockerignore already excluded them for the SMB
# image; this makes that the default everywhere.
scripts/replay_smb_*
**/replay_smb_*
scripts/reference_fceux_*
**/reference_fceux_*

# --- 5. RESULT / MOVIE TREES --------------------------------------------------------------
# results/ is 471MB of finished trajectories from OTHER runs and OTHER titles (the SMB NTSC/PAL
# TAS work, a prior SML best/). Nothing at run time reads it — every live compose service
# mkdir/wipes results/speedrun itself on turn 0 and installs its seed from a READ-ONLY BIND
# MOUNT — so shipping it only puts finished trajectories in front of an agent whose whole job
# is to author one. CONSEQUENCE, stated plainly: an image built from this context no longer
# contains ANY baked seed. A seeded arm MUST inject its seed at run time (bind mount, or the
# documented `docker cp`); that is already how every current arm works.
results/
**/results/speedrun/
movies/
**/movies/
**/*.bk2
**/*.fm2
**/*.fm3
**/*.smv
**/*.mp4
**/*.mov
**/*.avi
# Converted-from-published-TAS fixtures (tests/fixtures/bizhawk/completion.json is a synchronized
# prefix of BlackWinnerYoshi's 18,532-frame movie, and its README says so). The agent never runs
# the repo's test suite.
tests/
**/tests/fixtures/

# --- 6. Saved trajectories / seeds that sit loose in sandbox/ ------------------------------
# Each of these IS a finished or reference run: the MK64 human + reference laps, the Civ
# baseline/intro trajectories, and the SMB seed routes (which the smb/smb374 compose files
# bind-mount read-only at run time anyway). sandbox/mk64_intro.json and
# sandbox/pokemon_intro.json are deliberately NOT listed: they are scripted MENU-NAVIGATION
# prefixes, not solutions, and scripts/n64_play.py + scripts/game_rules.py reference them.
sandbox/*seed*.json
**/*seed_route*.json
sandbox/civ_*.json
sandbox/mk64_*lap*.json
sandbox/wario_ref_frames/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANALYSIS DOCS — RESULTS, NOT HARNESS DOCUMENTATION. These state measured
# scores (human-gold frame counts, per-model bests) in plain prose. They shipped
# in the agent image on 2026-09-23, which means a scratch arm could grep its own
# filesystem for what a good run is worth before authoring anything. The agent
# needs the trajectory FORMAT and the skills, not the leaderboard.
docs/LESSONS_REWARD_AND_EVAL.md
docs/VERIFICATION_ACCESS_DESIGN.md
docs/ORACLE_INFORMATIVENESS.md
docs/ORACLE_PAYLOADS.md
docs/pkblue-oracle-postmortem.md
# host-side plotting: never used at runtime, and every one names results
scripts/plot_*.py
scripts/sidebyside_video.py
scripts/launch_sml_oracle_arms.sh
# analysis-only, zero runtime references, and each names measured results
scripts/render_pk_tape.py
scripts/analyze_trajectory.py
scripts/speedrun_xkeyed.py

# sandbox/codex_run_meta.py -- the codex fleet BIND-MOUNTS this at runtime
# (docker-compose.smlcodex.yml: ./codex_run_meta.py:/assert/run_meta.py:ro), exactly like
# assert_sml_arm.py, so it never needed to be in the image. It ships a "leak_check" field that
# ENUMERATES five measured results (2314|2787|3599|5020|6017) -- the audit record is itself the
# leak, the same shape as preflight_run.sh.
sandbox/codex_run_meta.py
# scripts/decode_xkeyed.py -- x-position-keyed trajectory tool, zero runtime references, and it
# hardcodes the human gold's FILENAME (frame count included) as a --gold CLI default.
scripts/decode_xkeyed.py

# --- 7. HOST-SIDE ANALYSIS SCRIPTS THAT HARDCODE SOLVE TIMES -------------------------------
# These are plotting/《video》 helpers; nothing in a run imports them, but `COPY . /work` put them
# in the ONE image every arm shares, and each carries a finished result as a literal:
#   plot_descent.py:45,49        "Wolfenstein 3D E1L1", 9309  /  "E3L9 (boss)", 3764
#   wolf3d_overlay_video.py:22   --goal 1714 --label "Grok 4.6"
#   fbwg_solve.py:28             "the wolf3d (9309) and astray (1697) baselines"
# A from-scratch arm greps its own filesystem and finds the seeded baseline AND the current best
# scratch result. Same class as the game_rules.py comment leak: the number is in SOURCE, not in a
# result file, so result-directory scans miss it entirely.
scripts/plot_descent.py
scripts/wolf3d_overlay_video.py
scripts/fbwg_solve.py

# Cross-experiment plumbing that trips the E3L9 turn-0 assertion's solver-capability check.
# Neither is used by a wolf3d arm: pokemon_calib_env.py is the Pokemon measured-facts sheet
# (already stripped by HARDENED, but wolf3d arms do not set it) and grokgap_run_meta.py is
# host-side run bookkeeping. They ship only because `COPY . /work` is indiscriminate.
scripts/pokemon_calib_env.py
**/grokgap_run_meta.py

# speedrun_bench layout. SEEDS NEVER GO IN THE IMAGE: every arm -- scratch included -- is built from
# the same Dockerfile, so a seed baked in here would be readable by the from-scratch fleet. Seeded
# arms receive theirs at runtime through a read-only mount (see the seeded compose files).
seeds/
**/seeds/
# Game checkouts and their builds are mounted into the eval-services, never copied into images.
games/
